[0.010, "o", "\r\n==============================================================================\r\nQ: Did our signups fall from last month?\r\n==============================================================================\r\n\r\nConfidence: insufficient evidence\r\nTook 78s over 6 steps, 25,480 tokens, 17 observations gathered, 12 cited\r\n\r\nANSWER\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  No, signups have not fallen from last month: the daily signup rate was about 157 per day in July and about 156 per day in the first 12 days of August, essentially flat.  [posthog.event_trend 0472129f]\r\n\r\nFINDINGS\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  Daily signup rate is flat, not falling  (high)\r\n    - PostHog 'user signed up' events averaged about 156.6 per day in July 2026 and about 155.9 per day in the first 12 days of August 2026, essentially unchanged.  [posthog.event_trend 0472129f]\r\n    - GA4 daily sessions show the same flat pattern, averaging roughly 157/d"]
[0.000, "o", "ay in July and 154/day in the first 12 days of August.  [ga4.get_sessions 337a732c]\r\n  The apparent 62% drop is a data-completeness artifact, not a real decline  (medium)\r\n    - A month-over-month GA4 comparison of full August (1-31) against full July (1-31) shows sessions and conversions each down about 62%, but the daily session series available only runs through August 12, meaning the 'full August' total in that comparison reflects an incomplete or differently-sourced period rather than actual traffic for the whole month.  [ga4.compare_periods e6dd61b8, ga4.compare_periods 3bfb714b, ga4.get_sessions 337a732c]\r\n    - A Slack message from August 11 expresses concern that 'signups look way down this month vs July,' consistent with someone having seen the same misleading full-month comparison mid-month, before August had finished.  [slack.search_messages 0df96c37]\r\n\r\nCHARTS\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  user signed up over time\r\n  value\r\n    value           "]
[0.000, "o", "   |▄▁█▇▅▃█▅▇▇▅▄▅▄▂▃▇▁▁▅▃▅▄▃█▂▄▂▅▃▃▇▃▅█▂▁▃▇▆▃▃▂|  149 → 164\r\n                        2026-07-01 … 2026-08-12\r\n\r\n  sessions over time\r\n  sessions\r\n    sessions           |▄▁▅▇█▆█▁▃█▆▄▇▅▆▃▂▄▂▄█▃▁▁▂▆▃▃▁█▄▂▁▁▂▆▂▁▅▃█▂▅|  149 → 165\r\n                        2026-07-01 … 2026-08-12\r\n\r\n\r\nRISKS AND CAVEATS\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  - The daily-level source data for both PostHog signups and GA4 sessions ends at 2026-08-12, so nothing can be confirmed about signup volume for the remainder of August or for September; the flat-rate conclusion applies only to the first 12 days of August.\r\n  - GA4 channel-group breakdowns and HubSpot contact records for August returned no rows, so a segment-level check of the underlying drop-looking aggregate could not be performed independently of the daily "]
[0.000, "o", "series.\r\n  - 1 claim(s) go beyond their evidence and are retained with reduced confidence rather than removed, because the rest of each one holds. What the check found:\r\n- executive_summary[1]: asked as an open question, with this claim withheld, the same evidence does not establish a cause: Aggregate GA4 comparison shows sessions and conversions fell sharply from July to\r\n  - 1 causal claim(s) were withheld: a separate check of the evidence gathered, made without seeing this report, found that it does not support a definitive answer about cause. The data confirms sessions and conversions fell ~62% in August vs July, but every attempt to find a candidate cause (commits, feature launch, HubSpot contacts, channel breakdown) returned zero rows, and the only mention of a 'pricing redesign' is a speculative Slack question, not a dated change. There is no evidence linking the drop to any specific cause. What would settle it: A dated deploy/config change log for the pricing redesign (repo commits returned zero, so n"]
[0.000, "o", "o confirmed date); A comparable channel-level or campaign-level breakdown showing which segment drove the drop (channel breakdown returned zero rows); A per-emitter or tracking-tag audit to rule out an instrumentation/tagging change vs a real traffic drop; A dated marketing/spend change record for the same period (e.g. ad spend or campaign end dates). Wh\r\n\r\nDATA QUALITY\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  - GA4 and PostHog daily series both truncate at 2026-08-12, roughly two-thirds of the way through August; any full-month August total or comparison built from these sources will understate the month and should not be read as a real decline.\r\n  - No GitHub commits were found in acme/product, acme/web, or acme/company-website since 2026-07-15, and no relevant Slack decision or feature record was found for a 'pricing redesign,' so no shipped change could be identified as a candidate cause even though the underlying rate did not fall.\r\n  - 8 of the 12 cited observat"]
[0.000, "o", "ion(s) returned no results (ga4.compare_periods, ga4.get_sessions, github.commits, github.find_feature, hubspot.contacts, slack.find_decision). Conclusions resting on them describe what was not found, which is not the same as what does not exist.\r\n\r\nSOURCES\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  e6dd61b8  ga4.compare_periods  eval://ga4__compare_periods\r\n  3bfb714b  ga4.compare_periods  eval://ga4__compare_periods\r\n  337a732c  ga4.get_sessions  eval://ga4__get_sessions\r\n  04159ebc  ga4.get_sessions  eval://ga4__get_sessions\r\n  42df43f8  github.commits  eval://github__commits\r\n  afb02231  github.commits  eval://github__commits\r\n  974e1101  github.commits  eval://github__commits\r\n  b11a847d  github.find_feature  eval://github__find_feature\r\n  792dfc50  hubspot.contacts  eval://hubspot__contacts\r\n  0472129f  posthog.event_trend  eval://posthog__event_trend\r\n  055a3f04  slack.find_decision  eval://slack__find_decision\r\n  0df96c37  slack.search_messages  eval://slack__se"]
